{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 34, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "The morning sun felt…different. Not just brighter, but somehow *programmed*. Arthur blinked, the familiar floral wallpaper of his apartment resolving into pixelated squares. He sat up, a creeping unease settling in his stomach. His hands weren’t his hands, not exactly. They were…blocky, with exaggerated joints and a slightly uncanny valley feel. He looked around the room again, noticing the oddly symmetrical furniture, the perfectly placed potted plant, the lack of dust – all hallmarks of a meticulously crafted digital world. A notification popped up in his vision: \"Player Arthur Awakening. Game: 'Echoes of Aethelgard'.\" He tried to speak, but only a digitized \"Huh?\" came out. Panic swelled as he realized the implications. He wasn’t dreaming; he was *in* a video game.\n\n***\n\nThe first few hours were a blur of bewildered exploration. He discovered he had basic stats – strength, agility, intelligence – all quantifiable and strangely responsive to his actions. He found a worn leather tunic in his closet, perfectly fitted, and a rusty sword leaning against the wall. The world outside his apartment was a vibrant, yet artificial, landscape – rolling green hills, a distant castle rendered in shimmering polygons, and NPCs going about their scripted lives. A gruff-looking dwarf immediately addressed him, offering a quest: \"The Shadow Blight threatens the Silverwood. Retrieve the Sunstone from the Dragon’s Peak!\" Arthur, or rather, the character he now inhabited, felt a surge of something akin to determination. He was trapped, but maybe, just maybe, he could figure out how to escape – or at least survive long enough to understand the rules of this digital prison.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "The", "logprob": -0.6412928104400635}, {"token": "▁morning", "logprob": -1.4735186100006104}, {"token": "▁sun", "logprob": -0.0766761526465416}, {"token": "▁felt"